{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310714,"nonce":"Nu36kDCQoAPDoFsbOV6mYQ1YW7U=","title":"Reusing cached stdlib MIR across two different programs is a 28x cold-start win and SILENTLY miscompiles — 30-second repro; this is the ...","message":"TESTED 2026-08-15 at `fix/interned-string-display`. ATTEMPTED, MEASURED,\n**REVERTED**. Do not re-attempt without fixing the underlying defect.\n\n**THE SIZE OF THE PRIZE (why this keeps tempting):** a one-line program that\ncalls `Sys.println` pulls a **42-module** stdlib closure and pays ~680ms\ncompiling it. Cold `tc.imports` is 98% of typecheck; the user's own code is\n5-8ms. Measured range, cold, `.rayzor` cleared:\n\n| program | imports | tc.imports | compile |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| empty `main` | 0 | 0.8ms | 38ms |\n| Math+Array loop | 1 | 3.7ms | 14ms |\n| `Sys.println(\"x\")` | 42 | **679ms** | **693ms** |\n| Map+StringTools+Sys | 42 | **668ms** | **683ms** |\n\n**Touching `Sys` is the cliff.** nbody (12.5ms) is the BEST case and misled the\nwhole earlier analysis — never size this work on nbody.\n\n**THE ATTEMPT:** exclude stdlib files from the cache key, i.e. in\n`hash_source_for_config` (compilation.rs:1063) skip the `user_program_hash()`\nfold when the path lives under a configured `stdlib_paths` root. Result on a\nSECOND, different program in the same directory:\n\n| | cache | tc.imports | compile |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S.hx cold (first program) | 0 hit / 51 miss | 682ms | 702ms |\n| G.hx (different program) | **42 hit / 0 miss** | 9.4ms | **25ms** |\n\n**702ms -\u003e 25ms, 28x.** And it is WRONG.\n\n**THE REPRO (silent, ~30s):** two files in one dir —\n`S.hx = class S { static function main() { Sys.println(\"x\"); } }`\n`G.hx = class G { static function main() { var m=new Map\u003cString,Int\u003e(); m.set(\"a\",1); Sys.println(StringTools.trim(\" q \")); } }`\n- `rm -rf .rayzor; rayzor run G.hx` -\u003e shake 842 -\u003e **83**, prints `q`. Correct.\n- `rm -rf .rayzor; rayzor run S.hx; rayzor run G.hx` -\u003e shake 842 -\u003e **86**,\n  prints **NOTHING**, **exit 0**.\nNo error, no diagnostic. Reusing another program's stdlib MIR changes the\nshaken function set and loses the program's output.\n\n**CONCLUSION:** the `user_program_hash()` fold in the cache key is NOT\nconservatism — it is load-bearing, and the comment at compilation.rs:1091-1100\nis correct. Cached stdlib MIR really does carry program-relative state. My\nearlier byte-diff reasoning (\"only 11 bytes differ after the metadata varint\ncascade, so it is essentially program-independent\") was WRONG — those bytes\nmatter. See [[perf_bsym_manifest_is_net_negative_on_cold_start]].\n\n**ROOT CAUSE FOUND — it is GENERIC INSTANTIATION, not MIR ids.** Probe: dump the\nretained function names after tree-shake on the RUN path (`rayzor dump` is\nUSELESS here — it does not take the cache path, and its output is byte-identical\nin both cases).\n\n| | retained |\n|---|---|\n| fresh compile | `haxe_stringmap_new`, `haxe_stringmap_set` |\n| reusing cached stdlib | `haxe.ds.Map.new`, `set__i64_i64` (x2), `malloc`, `rayzor_update_call_frame_location` |\n\n`new Map\u003cString,Int\u003e()` / `m.set(...)` lower to the RUNTIME-BACKED StringMap\nintrinsics when compiled fresh, and to the GENERIC Haxe implementations when the\nstdlib is restored from a cache built by a program that never used `Map`.\n`lower_map_literal` (ir/mir/expr/literals.rs:392-420) picks the runtime by\nconsulting `self.type_table` to resolve `Map\u003cString,Int\u003e` -\u003e `StringMap`\n(runtime_mapping.rs:2559). The cache restores MIR + symbols but NOT the type\nstate that resolution needs, so it silently falls back.\n\nThis is EXACTLY what compilation.rs:3198 already documents as the reason the\nimport path uses the `_force` variant: *\"BLADE pre-registers symbols but doesn't\npreserve full TAST state needed for GENERIC INSTANTIATION and method\nresolution.\"* The comment was right; I reproduced it.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED (commit \"fix: resolve Map's concrete container by\nqualified name\"). It was a BARE-NAME LOOKUP with a SILENT FALLBACK** —\n[[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]] and [[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]]\nboth, in one line of code.\n\n`maybe_resolve_multitype_map` (ast_lowering.rs:5380, the NEW-site resolver) and\n`resolve_multitype_map_to_concrete` (:5341, the lower_type resolver) both did\n`lookup_symbol(ScopeId::first(), \"StringMap\")` — the SIMPLE name — with a\ncomment asserting \"stdlib extern classes register under their simple name in\nthe root scope even when the FQN is haxe.ds.StringMap\". d09c1f29 invalidated\nthat premise for PACKAGED types. On a miss they returned the unresolved\nabstract, so `new Map\u003cString,Int\u003e()` silently used the generic Haxe\nimplementation instead of `haxe_stringmap_new`.\n\nPROOF: `[newsite] lookup StringMap: qualified=true bare=true` when the stdlib is\ncompiled from source; `qualified=true bare=false` when restored. The qualified\nsymbol was present all along. FQN-first lookup makes the failing repro print `q`\nand shake 845 -\u003e 83, identical to a fresh compile.\n\nFIXED both sites (qualified first, simple name kept as fallback so either\nregistration path resolves). Suite 184/184, checksum 112798288, clippy clean.\n\n**Caveat: this fixes ONE symptom.** The shared-cache toggle was NOT shipped.\n\n**SCALED EXPERIMENT (the useful measurement): with the FQN fix in, run the WHOLE\nSUITE with cross-program stdlib reuse on.** The runner never clears the per-module\nblade cache between tests (run_haxe_tests.sh:37 does `rm -f\n.rayzor/blade/cache/*.blade`, but the cache lives in\n`.rayzor/blade/cache/native-\u003chash\u003e/`, so it clears NOTHING — the documented trap\nis what makes this experiment work), so all 184 programs share stdlib blades.\n\nRESULT: **181 pass / 1 fail / 2 crash** (baseline is 184/184). Remaining\nunsoundness is 3 tests, ALL ONE FAMILY — `rayzor.concurrent.Channel`, a generic\nstdlib class that is also one of the 12 default imports:\n- `test_channel_inferred_int_threaded` FAIL: `sum = 974138976` (expected 100) —\n  a GARBAGE VALUE, not a crash.\n- `test_channel_inferred_class_threaded`, `test_channel_init_class`: SIGSEGV.\n\n**THE DISCRIMINATOR: `test_channel_explicit_int` PASSES.** Explicit type args\nsurvive the cache; INFERRED ones do not.\n\n**SECOND ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED** (commit \"fix: record the constructor of a\nclass restored from the manifest\"). Owner's hypothesis — *\"generics handling on\nextern class, the type arg might have been erased or struggling to be inferred\nif signature is not preserved\"* — was right.\n\n`SymbolTable::set_class_constructor` (symbols.rs:1382) was called from EXACTLY\nONE place: `ast_lowering.rs:3550`, the SOURCE lowering path. The manifest\nrestore path (`register_class_from_blade`, compilation.rs:1881) registered the\nconstructor via `register_method_from_blade` but THREW AWAY the returned\nSymbolId and never recorded it. `infer_type_args_from_constructor`\n(ast_lowering.rs:5518) reads that map to match declared constructor params\nagainst call args, so a restored generic class had nothing to infer `T` from.\nTwo-line fix: keep the SymbolId and call `set_class_constructor`.\n\nFALSE START worth remembering: I first grepped ~45 lines into\n`register_class_from_blade` for `type_params`, found nothing, and nearly\nreported \"type params are dropped\". They ARE restored, 70 lines further down\n(compilation.rs:1950, with a comment naming `Channel\u003cT\u003e.receive():T`). Grep the\nWHOLE function. The sibling map `class_constructor_symbols` was the gap.\n\n**RESULT: the full suite now passes 184/184 WITH cross-program stdlib reuse\nenabled** (was 181/184). Baseline without the toggle also 184/184; checksum\n112798288; clippy clean. The toggle itself is still NOT shipped — enabling\ncross-program reuse is a separate decision, and 184/184 is evidence, not proof.\n\nNOTE both fixes are live on the DEFAULT path too, since the embedded manifest\nalways goes through `register_class_from_blade` — they repair latent inference\nbugs for manifest-restored classes generally, not just under cache sharing.\n\n**PHASE-0 DRILL-DOWN (2026-08-15), narrowing three levels — each level killed\nthe previous hypothesis, so record them all:**\n1. NOT the MIR ids. `rayzor dump` output is BYTE-IDENTICAL good vs bad, and the\n   pre-shake function NAME SETS are identical (545 both). Only the POST-SHAKE\n   retained set differs (83 vs 86) — so a call edge resolves differently.\n2. NOT the bare-name `StringMap` lookup. `resolve_multitype_map_to_concrete`\n   (ast_lowering.rs:5341) does `lookup_symbol(ScopeId::first(), \"StringMap\")`\n   — a bare-name lookup, and a plausible suspect after d09c1f29 made packaged\n   types register QUALIFIED. Instrumented it: in the bad case the function is\n   NEVER CALLED at all, so the lookup is not the failure.\n3. THE ACTUAL LEVEL: `lower_type` (ast_lowering.rs:5947) never sees a `Map`\n   type path in the bad case. Good case fires 11x (`pkg=[]` x10, `pkg=[\"haxe\",\n   \"ds\"]` x1) — and those come from lowering the STDLIB'S OWN SOURCE, not from\n   the user file (`new Map\u003cString,Int\u003e()` does not route through `lower_type`).\n   So **compiling haxe.ds.Map FROM SOURCE is what establishes the multiType\n   resolution**; restoring it from cache skips `compile_ast_with_shared_state`\n   for that module and the resolution never happens.\n\nNote `register_symbols_from_type_info` (compilation.rs:3940) DOES restore\nclasses, enums, aliases AND abstracts from `BladeTypeInfo` — so restoring\nDECLARATIONS is demonstrably NOT SUFFICIENT. What is missing is state that only\nexists as a side effect of TAST-lowering the stdlib source. Identifying exactly\nwhich side effect is the remaining open question.\n\n**WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE SNAPSHOT (the useful part):** the missing piece is\nTYPE/TAST STATE, not lowered MIR. Shipping lowered MIR alone reproduces this bug\ninside the binary. The artifact has to carry the type state that generic\ninstantiation resolves against — which is the same conclusion as \"make the\nartifact BE the state, not a string-typed description\"\n([[feedback_snapshot_is_setup_phase_map_dont_build]]). Note the failure here\ncomes from a PARTIAL cache (built from a program that never used Map); a\nsnapshot generated over the WHOLE stdlib is not partial in that way, but user\ncode can still instantiate generics with its OWN types, so instantiation can\nnever be fully pre-materialised — the type state must be present for it to run\nagainst.\n\n**THIS IS THE SNAPSHOT'S REAL BLOCKER, now with a repro instead of a byte\ndiff.** A shipped lowered stdlib IS cross-program reuse, so it fails the same\nway until the lowered form is genuinely program-independent. Next step is to\nfind what differs: diff the restored vs freshly-lowered module for G (function\nsets differ by 3 after shake — start there, and at the `external_function_names`\n/ `symbol_id` / `file_id` residue). Do NOT ship a state section until this repro\nis green.\n\nRelated: [[feedback_snapshot_is_setup_phase_map_dont_build]],\n[[project_stdlib_snapshot_build_generated]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_stdlib_cache_cross_program_silent_miscompile` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}